  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday morning through next Thursday)\nIssued at 153 PM EDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nA quiet long-term forecast is in store with dominant surface high-\npressure conditions keeping the sensible weather dry and cool \nthrough the weekend. At the mid-upper levels, our region is between \na weak low transiting along the Carolina coast and an amplified \nridge building in toward the ArkLaTex region. The primary effect of \nthis will be a renewed development of the CAD pattern and a \nstrengthening of the northeast winds over the area through early \nnext week. As a result, some gusty winds will be possible through \nthe weekend, peaking in the afternoon--particularly south of the I-\n85 interchange around 15-20mph each day. Otherwise, a few more \nclouds may be around, but things will remain very dry with no chance \nof rain through the end of next week. Along with these dry \nconditions, we will continue to keep an eye on our RH values through \nthe forecast period in case a Fire Danger Statement is warranted. \n\nTemperatures will be near seasonal averages to slightly below for \nthe weekend before a gradual warming trend begins as we move into \nearly next week, with daytime highs rising into the mid to upper 70s \nrange by mid-week. Additionally, models are in agreement beyond mid-\nweek for the upper-level pattern to become more zonal, setting up \nthe potential for a reintroduction of southwesterly flow over the \nregion. The jury is still out, however, on whether this translates \ninto any meaningful rainfall, as models are currently split on the \nextent of Gulf moisture influx as we transition into late next\nweek.",
